{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-149492",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $B$ be the event that a child can play in all positions, $I$ be the event that a child has been playing for over a year, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a child is in team $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.54 &  & P(I|{T}_{1})=0.2 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.32 &  & P(I|{T}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.14 &  & P(I|{T}_{3})=0.15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.54+0.32+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of children can play in all positions, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $94%$ of children who play in all positions have been playing for over a year, we have\n\n$$\nP(I|B)=0.94.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child who has been playing for over a year can play in all positions, which is represented as $P(B|I).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|I) & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(I|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(I|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.94)(0.1)}{(0.2)(0.54)+(0.3)(0.32)+(0.15)(0.14)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.094}{0.225} \\\\  & ≈0.418,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
